🎯 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“You cannot SSH into a production server. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I would not immediately assume that the server is down.

First, I want to identify where the SSH connection is failing.

I would start with:

ssh user@server-ip

If it fails, I would check whether the server is reachable:

ping <server-ip>

If ping is blocked, I wouldn't use that alone to conclude the server is unreachable.

Next, I would check whether SSH port 22 is accessible:

nc -vz <server-ip> 22

or:

telnet <server-ip> 22

Then I would verify the SSH service on the server, if I have console or alternate access:

systemctl status sshd

I would also check whether SSH is actually listening:

ss -lntp | grep :22

Then I would investigate:

• Is the server reachable?
• Is port 22 open?
• Is sshd running?
• Is the firewall blocking the connection?
• Is the security group/network ACL allowing SSH?
• Is the username correct?
• Is the SSH key/permission correct?
• Was there a recent configuration change?

For detailed client-side troubleshooting, I can use:

ssh -vvv user@server-ip

This gives me verbose SSH connection details and helps identify where the connection is failing.

If authentication is the problem, I would verify the key and permissions rather than changing random settings.

For example:

chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_rsa

Once the issue is identified and corrected, I would test the connection again and verify normal server access.

Finally, I would document:

Issue → Investigation → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

When SSH fails, don't simply say “SSH is not working.”

Break the problem down:

𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗰𝗵𝗮𝗯𝗶𝗹𝗶𝘁𝘆 → 𝗣𝗼𝗿𝘁 → 𝗦𝗦𝗛 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲 → 𝗙𝗶𝗿𝗲𝘄𝗮𝗹𝗹 → 𝗔𝘂𝘁𝗵𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 → 𝗣𝗲𝗿𝗺𝗶𝘀𝘀𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀

That's how you approach a production troubleshooting problem systematically. 🚀

